  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nTemperatures will gradually warm to above normal temperatures by the \nmiddle of this week due to a mid/upper level ridge of high pressure \nand a modifying airmass. \n\nDeep mid and upper level moisture associated with a shortwave moving \nacross South Texas will continue to bring a very low chance of light \nrain through early tonight. Chances for rain will increase along the \ncoast and over the waters as a surface trough develops, streaming \nlow level moisture into the area Wednesday. As the trough shifts to \nthe west, low rain chances will slide west over the southern Coastal \nPlains. High pressure will then become more dominate late this week, \nreducing rain chances to nearly zero through the weekend.\n\nNortheast to east winds are expected to continue through Thursday. \nEven with winds only 5-15 knots, the persistent direction and swell \nperiods around 8 seconds could lead to minor coastal flooding during \ntimes of high tide. As mentioned previously, guidance does keep tide \nlevels below the 2 ft MSL threshold, but will continue to monitor \nconditions. \n\nAlthough the northeast flow tends to limit the rip current risk to a \nlow risk, the 8 second period swells makes it hard to go with a low \nrisk. Have left the moderate rip current risk as moderate through \nTuesday.",
